  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "2.0.0 — the standards release.\n\n* Full OAuth 2.1 / OpenID Connect protocol core: discovery, JWKS with two-phase key rotation,\n  authorization code + PKCE (S256 only, required for public clients), refresh token rotation,\n  client credentials, device authorization grant (RFC 8628), pushed authorization requests\n  (RFC 9126), introspection (RFC 7662), revocation (RFC 7009), dynamic client registration\n  (RFC 7591/7592), RP-initiated logout, and issuer identification (RFC 9207).\n* Access tokens now follow RFC 9068 (typ: at+jwt, client_id, jti, scope).\n* Codes and refresh tokens are stored as SHA-256 hashes; replay of either is treated as compromise\n  and revokes the derived tokens or the whole refresh-token family.\n* Per-client setup page at /{tenant}/oauth2/integrate/{client_id}, generated from the client's own\n  registration, with copy-paste config for the Ark client, the raw ASP.NET Core handler,\n  oidc-client-ts, Authlib and go-oidc.\n* Sign-in and consent pages load no third-party resources and work under a strict CSP.\n\nBreaking / migration: the implicit grant, the hybrid flow and the resource owner password\ncredentials grant are not supported. The v1 /oauth/{tenant}/v1/... routes still work and keep their\nrequest and response shapes, but now delegate to the protocol core — so codes issued there are\nsingle-use, expire, and have their PKCE verifier checked. Existing databases must run migration\n00003 (GET /api/migration/v1/sql?action=up&name=00003_sql.sql), which adds the protocol state\ntables and RFC 7591 metadata columns, seeds the scope catalogue, and adopts each tenant's existing\nRSA key as its active signing key with kid = tenant_id, so tokens already issued keep validating.\n\nPackaging: the package no longer ships application content or static web assets into consuming\nprojects, and now carries a readme, an SPDX license expression and a symbol package.",
